  "narrative": "During the 1950s, Black and African women's hairstyles were deeply rooted in their cultural heritage and identity. Although specific details about hairstyles during this decade are scarce, it can be inferred that styles such as cornrows, a traditional African braiding practice, may have been prevalent among these communities. Cornrows were likely worn close to the scalp, using an underhand motion to create continuous, raised rows. The natural texture of kinky hair was also present in many Black and African women's hairstyles during this time, with some individuals embracing their natural growth or styling it into distinctive curls. Unfortunately, documentation from this era is limited, but it is clear that hair culture played a significant role in the lives of Black and African women, serving as an expression of their identity, community, and heritage.",
